### Step 1 – Reduction to the triangle \(T_k\)

> “Replace each non‑sunny line by a vertical line that passes through the leftmost column not yet occupied by a vertical line. A vertical line covers **all** points of a column, whereas a horizontal line or a line of slope \(-1\) can meet a column in at most one point. Hence after the replacement we still cover every point of \(S_n\) and we have exactly the same number \(k\) of sunny lines.”

**Analysis:**  
The intention is to show that any configuration with \(k\) sunny lines can be transformed into one where the \(n-k\) non‑sunny lines are vertical lines \(x=1,\dots ,x=n-k\).  
The argument assumes that after each replacement the points in the *remaining* columns are already covered by the sunny lines. This is not proved: a non‑sunny line (horizontal or slope \(-1\)) may cover points in a column that later becomes a “right‑most” column (i.e. with \(x>n-k\)), and those points would no longer be covered after the replacement. A rigorous proof would have to show that one can always choose the replacement columns so that every point in the columns \(>n-k\) is already covered by sunny lines, which is omitted.  

**Classification:** *Justification Gap* – the reduction is plausible but not rigorously justified.

